# PREPARED RIBBONS LAYOUTS: HOW TO READ THIS DATA
# This module holds 24 pictures, f0 through f23. Each tilemap is a nested list:
# 16 rows of 20 tile handles, read left-to-right and then top-to-bottom.
# 20 * 8 = 160 pixels wide; 16 * 8 = 128 pixels high. draw_map places it at
# tile row 1, leaving the first and last screen rows for the main scene labels.
#
# A list item such as tiles.t43 refers to a pattern in tiles.pysn. It is not a
# new sprite and does not run a drawing command by itself. tilemap() converts
# this static arrangement into ROM asset data during compilation. Repeated
# handles reuse pixel patterns. Runtime draw() chooses one of these layouts;
# the console does not recompute the original shaded ribbons image.
#
# The long rows are authored asset data. To change motion, change the frame
# sequence or timing; to redesign the images, edit the layouts and/or atlas.
# You do not need to hand-enter lists like these for normal movement: a BMP
# tilemap or a handful of sprites can be much easier for a basic game.

